    --> copy help text to $HOME/dbxl.help.text


1. Introduction to dbxl
-----------------------

dbxl is a graphical source-level debugger for the X Window System. It shows
a program's source, its variables, the call stack, breakpoints, machine
instructions, registers and memory in a set of windows inside one frame, and
lets you control the program with the mouse and a few keys.

dbxl follows the look and behaviour of IBM's xldb debugger for AIX closely.
Its windows, menus and dialogs work the way xldb's did, so what you learned
there applies here.

A typical session:

    dbxl ./myprogram arg1 arg2

dbxl starts the program under the debugger and stops it at the first line
of main. The Source window shows where the program stopped, marked by an
arrow in the left margin. From there you can:

    - left-click a source line to set or clear a breakpoint;
    - left-click Continue, Next, Step or Return in the Commands window;
    - left-click a variable in the Locals or Globals window to change how it
      is shown, to change its value or to monitor it.

To leave dbxl, left-click Exit in the Commands window.


2. About this version
---------------------

This section describes what is particular to this version of dbxl.
    --> Linux and GDB
    --> Scaling for high resolution screens
    --> Differences from xldb


2.1 Linux and GDB
-----------------

dbxl runs on Linux and uses GDB to control the program being debugged. GDB
must be installed and must have Python support (version 14 or later). The
environment variable DBXL_GDB names a different GDB program to use.

The program runs on the terminal dbxl was started from: its output appears
there, and it reads its input from there. While the program runs, Ctrl-C on
that terminal stops it.

Registers and machine instructions are those of the machine dbxl runs on.
On x86-64 the Registers window shows the registers described in
    --> The Registers window


2.2 Scaling for high resolution screens
---------------------------------------

dbxl draws everything in a fixed-size font, as xldb did. On a high
resolution screen this can be too small to read. The -scale option enlarges
everything by a whole factor:

    dbxl -scale 2 ./myprogram

-scale auto picks the largest factor for which the frame still fits the
screen. The factor can also be set with the resource dbxl.scale.


2.3 Differences from xldb
-------------------------

dbxl aims to look and behave like xldb. Where it differs, it is on purpose:

    - dbxl debugs Linux programs with GDB; registers and disassembly are
      those of the host machine.
    - Windows are always redrawn completely; xldb sometimes left fragments
      of closed dialogs on the screen.
    - With -bw and -wb, the arrow and stop signs use the scheme's colours so
      that they stay visible.
    - Addresses are shown with as many digits as the program's pointers.


3. The dbxl windows
-------------------

All dbxl windows live inside one frame. A window can be open, minimized to a
small title (called a chip), or hidden. Left-click a chip to open its
window. Each window has a title bar; left-clicking it opens the Window
Control menu.
    --> Window control

Moving the mouse into a window brings it to the front, unless autoraise is
turned off in the Options menu. The middle mouse button raises a window. The
right mouse button moves or resizes it.


3.1 Moving within windows
-------------------------

The mouse pointer is dbxl's cursor: keys that move "the cursor" move the
pointer, scrolling the window when needed.

    Cursor keys : move the cursor by one character or line.
    Page Up     : scrolls the window up one page.
    Page Down   : scrolls the window down one page.
    Home        : moves the cursor to the first column.
    End         : moves the cursor to the end of the line.
    :n<Enter>   : moves the cursor to line n.
    /x<Enter>   : moves the cursor forward to the next occurrence of x.
    \x<Enter>   : moves the cursor backward to the previous occurrence of x.
    ?x<Enter>   : the same as \x.
    /<Enter>    : searches forward again for the last string.
    \<Enter>    : searches backward again for the last string.

Typing :, /, \ or ? opens a small input line in the window; Enter carries
out the command and Escape cancels it. Whether searches match upper and
lower case exactly is set by "Case Sensitive" in the Options menu.


3.2 Source, Disassembly and Storage scrolling
---------------------------------------------

Use the scroll bars, the keys described in
    --> Moving within windows
or the arrows on the scroll bars to scroll these windows. When the program
stops, the Source and Disassembly windows scroll so that the stop line is
in the middle of the window.


3.3 Window control
------------------

Left-click a window's title bar to open the Window Control menu:

    Restore                : returns a maximized window to its size.
    Move                   : moves the window; drag its outline.
    Size                   : resizes the window; drag its edges.
    Minimize               : replaces the window by its chip.
    Maximize               : makes the window fill the frame.
    Lower                  : puts the window behind the others.
    Horizontal scroll bars : shows or hides the horizontal scroll bar.
    Vertical scroll bars   : shows or hides the vertical scroll bar.
    Save Window            : writes the window's text to a file.

You can also drag a window with the right mouse button: pressing near the
middle moves it, pressing near an edge or a corner resizes it.

To keep a layout, use "Save layout" in the Options menu and copy the file
it writes into your .Xdefaults file.
    --> Configuring dbxl using .Xdefaults


3.4 The Breakpoints window
--------------------------

The Breakpoints window lists the breakpoints in the order they were set:

    Subprogram [line LineNumber in File]

A conditional breakpoint also shows the object it tests and its trigger,
and a disabled breakpoint is marked "Disabled".

Left-click a breakpoint to show its line in the Source window and to open
the "Select Breakpoint Action" menu:

    Clear breakpoint         : removes the breakpoint.
    Disable breakpoint       : keeps it but lets the program pass.
    Enable breakpoint        : makes a disabled breakpoint stop again.
    Clear all breakpoints    : removes all breakpoints (after asking).
    Disable all breakpoints  : disables every breakpoint.
    Enable all breakpoints   : enables every breakpoint.

Breakpoints are shown in the left margin of the Source and Disassembly
windows as stop signs; a hollow one is disabled, and one with a question
mark is conditional.


3.5 The Callers window
----------------------

The Callers window lists the calls that led to the current stop, the
innermost first. The highlighted line is the frame shown in the other
windows.

Left-click a caller to look at it: the Locals window shows its variables,
and the Source and Disassembly windows show the line (and instruction) of
the call, highlighted. The arrow stays where the program stopped.


3.6 The Commands window
-----------------------

    Continue      : runs the program until a breakpoint or a signal.
    Next          : runs to the next source line, stepping over calls.
    Step          : runs to the next source line, stepping into calls.
    Machine step  : runs one machine instruction.
    Return        : runs until the current subprogram returns.
    Signal        : (not yet available)
    Edit          : edits the file shown in the Source window.
    Restart       : starts the program again from the beginning.
    Exit          : leaves dbxl (after asking).
    Breakpoint    : sets a breakpoint at a subprogram you name.
    Options       : opens the Options menu.
    --> Options
    Help          : opens this help.

Each command can also be given with the Alt key shown next to it, when the
mouse pointer is in any dbxl window. The list of commands can be changed
with the resource dbxl.commandList.


3.7 The Disassembly window
--------------------------

The Disassembly window shows the machine instructions of the subprogram
where the program stopped, one per line:

    address (+offset)  instruction operands

The arrow marks the next instruction to run. Left-click an instruction to
set or clear a breakpoint there. "Machine step" runs one instruction.


3.8 The Files window
--------------------

The Files window lists the program's source files. Left-click one to show
it in the Source window.


3.9 The Globals window
----------------------

The Globals window shows the program's global and static variables, file by
file. Left-click a variable to change how it is shown, to change its value
or to monitor it.
    --> Displaying variables and registers


3.10 The Help window
--------------------

The Help window shows this text. Left-click a line starting with "-->" to go
to that section. The title bar has three buttons:

    [exit]   : closes the Help window.
    [index]  : shows the help index.
    [return] : goes back to where you were before the last jump.


3.11 The Locals window
----------------------

The Locals window shows the parameters and local variables of the selected
subprogram, titled with its name and file. Which variables are shown is set
by "Local variables" in the Options menu.
    --> Displaying variables and registers


3.12 The Monitor window
-----------------------

The Monitor window collects variables you want to keep an eye on. Left-click
a variable in the Locals or Globals window and select "Monitor on/off" to
add it here or to remove it again.


3.13 The Storage window
-----------------------

The Storage window shows memory, 16 bytes per line: the address, four words
in hex and the same bytes as characters. It starts at the program's data.

Left-click a word to open a menu:

    Edit         : changes the word in place; type hex digits and Enter,
                   or Escape to leave it unchanged.
    Breakpoint   : (not yet available)
    Storage view : scrolls the window so that the word is the first line.

"Storage view" in a variable's menu scrolls the Storage window to that
variable (or, for a register, to the address it holds).


3.14 The Messages window
------------------------

The Messages window is a single line across the bottom of the frame. It
shows messages from dbxl and disappears at the next mouse click.


3.15 The Registers window
-------------------------

The Registers window shows the machine registers in groups. On x86-64:

    Special : RIP EFLAGS CS SS DS ES FS GS FS_BASE GS_BASE MXCSR
    General : RAX RBX RCX RDX RSI RDI RBP RSP R8 to R15
    Double  : the low 64 bits of XMM0 to XMM15
    Float   : the low 32 bits of XMM0 to XMM15

Which groups are shown is set by "Register control" in the Options menu.
Left-click a register to change its style or value, or to set a conditional
breakpoint on it.


3.16 The Source window
----------------------

The Source window shows the file of the selected subprogram. The arrow in
the left margin marks the line where the program stopped; stop signs mark
breakpoints. Left-click a line to set or clear a breakpoint on it.

dbxl finds source files with the source search path, which you can change
with "Edit source search path" in the Options menu and with -I.


3.17 The Subprograms window
---------------------------

The Subprograms window lists the program's subprograms. Left-click one to
show it in the Source and Disassembly windows. "Subprograms" in the Options
menu chooses between those with debugging information and all of them.


3.18 The Threads window
-----------------------

The Threads window lists the program's threads; the highlighted one is the
thread that stopped.


4. Program variables and registers
----------------------------------

The Locals, Globals and Monitor windows show variables as

    name: value

Left-click a variable, or any part of a structured value, to open a menu of
things you can do with it. The menu lists display styles, levels of detail
and actions that suit the object's type.
    --> Legend
    --> Displaying variables and registers
    --> Changing variables and registers
    --> Setting conditional breakpoints on variables and registers


4.1 Legend
----------

    +12, -3        a signed integer
    129            an unsigned integer
    'b', '\0'      a character
    1.5e+0         a floating-point number
    GREEN          an enumeration value
    ptr, <null>    a pointer, and a null pointer
    ->shape{}      a pointer followed to its (structure) target
    []             an array, not expanded
    shape{}        a structure, not expanded
    [ a b c ]      an array, one level expanded
    { a b c }      a structure, one level expanded


4.2 Displaying variables and registers
--------------------------------------

Detail:
    more    : shows one more level of an array, structure or pointer.
    less    : shows one level less.
    flatten : like more, but keeps a structure on one line.
The number of levels per click is set by "Detail per click" in the Options
menu.

Style:
    character, signed, unsigned, hex : integers.
    decimal, scientific, hex         : floating-point numbers.
    string, array, pointer           : what a pointer points to.
    address : the object's address.
    type    : the object's type.
    size    : the object's size in bytes.
    save    : remembers this object's style.
    recall  : gives this object the remembered style.
    default : goes back to the object's normal style.

Other actions:
    Select subrange    : shows only some elements of an array, or shows the
                         elements a pointer points to, as [low..high].
    Cast               : changes the type a pointer points to.
    Monitor on/off     : adds the object to the Monitor window, or removes
                         it.
    Storage view       : shows the object in the Storage window.
    Function parameter : uses the object in the next user command.


4.3 Changing variables and registers
------------------------------------

Select "Edit" in an object's menu, change the value and press Enter (or
left-click "proceed"). The value is read in the object's current style:

    signed      : an optional sign and decimal digits, such as -41.
    unsigned    : decimal digits, such as 12.
    hex         : hex digits, optionally after 0x, such as 0x4b64.
    character   : a quoted character, such as 'a', '\n' or '\x41'.
    decimal and scientific : a floating-point number such as -1.25e-3,
                  or inf or nan.

If the value does not fit the style, dbxl says what it expects and asks
again.


4.4 Setting conditional breakpoints on variables and registers
---------------------------------------------------------------

Select "Breakpoint" in an object's menu to stop at the current line only
when the object's value matches a trigger:

    X      : the value is X.
    !X     : the value is not X.
    X..Y   : the value is between X and Y (inclusive).
    !X..Y  : the value is outside X..Y.

Values are read in the object's current style. A conditional breakpoint is
shown as a stop sign with a question mark.


5. Options
----------

Left-click Options in the Commands window to open the Options menu.
Left-click an item to change it; the menu stays open.

    Save layout               : writes the current window layout to a file.
    Edit source search path   : changes where source files are looked for.
    Local variables           : Active shows the variables in scope; All
                                shows every variable of the subprogram.
    Detail per click          : left-click decreases, right-click increases
                                the levels that more and less change.
    Multiprocess debugging    : (not yet available)
    Fork path                 : (not yet available)
    Autoraise                 : whether windows come to the front when the
                                mouse enters them.
    Compiler variables        : whether compiler-made variables are shown.
    Language                  : Automatic.
    Register control          : which register groups are shown.
    Subprograms               : With -g only, or All.
    Save Breakpoints          : writes the breakpoints to a file.
    Load Breakpoints          : sets the breakpoints in a file.
    Case Sensitive            : whether searches match case exactly.
    Breakpoint all Subprograms: sets a breakpoint at the start of every
                                subprogram with debugging information.


6. Saving and restoring breakpoints
-----------------------------------

"Save Breakpoints" in the Options menu writes the current breakpoints to a
file, by default .dbxl.name in the current directory, where name is the
program's name. The resource dbxl.breakpointsFile sets another default.

"Load Breakpoints" sets the breakpoints saved in a file, adding them to the
ones already set.

With the resource dbxl.loadBreakpoints set to true, dbxl loads the saved
breakpoints when it starts the program; -k prevents that.


A. How do I ...?
----------------

    set a breakpoint on a line:
        Left-click the line in the Source window.
    set a breakpoint on a subprogram:
        Left-click Breakpoint in the Commands window and type its name.
    see a variable in hex:
        Left-click it and select "hex".
    see the elements of an array a pointer points to:
        Left-click the pointer and select "Select subrange".
    change a variable:
        Left-click it and select "Edit".
    stop when a variable has a certain value:
        Left-click it and select "Breakpoint".
    make the text bigger:
        Start dbxl with -scale 2 (or -scale auto).
    keep my window layout:
        Select "Save layout" in the Options menu and copy the file into
        your .Xdefaults.


B. Command-line invocation
--------------------------

    dbxl [options] [program [arguments ...]]

    -display Display   : the X server to use.
    -name Name         : the name used to look up resources.
    -font Font         : the font (default 8x13).
    -geometry Geometry : the frame's size and position.
    -title Title       : the frame's title.
    -bg Color          : the background colour.
    -fg Color          : the foreground colour.
    -bw                : black on white.
    -wb                : white on black.
    -scale Factor      : auto, 1, 2, 3 or 4.
    -E Command         : the edit command (%s is the file, %d the line).
    -I Directory       : adds Directory to the source search path.
    -k                 : does not load saved breakpoints.
    -h                 : prints this help and exits.


C. Configuring dbxl using .Xdefaults
------------------------------------

dbxl reads resources from the X server and from $HOME/.Xdefaults, as
name.resource, where name is dbxl (or the -name option). For example:

    dbxl.font: 8x13
    dbxl.scale: 2
    dbxl.autoRaise: false

Colours: background, foreground, titleBackground, titleForeground,
borderActive, borderIdle, dialogBackground, dialogForeground,
menuInfoForeground, markBackground, markForeground, cursorForeground,
mouseBody, mouseOutline, scrollButtonIdle; blackOnWhite and whiteOnBlack.

Behaviour: autoRaise, confirmExit, tagWindows, commandList, runTo, edit,
breakpointsFile, loadBreakpoints.

Layout: with specialLayout set to true, dbxl uses these resources for each
window W (Breakpoints, Callers, Commands, Disassembly, Files, Formats,
Globals, Help, Locals, Messages, Monitor, Registers, Source, Storage,
Subprograms, Threads):

    dbxl.WGeometry: WidthxHeight+X+Y
    dbxl.WIconGeometry: WidthxHeight+X+Y
    dbxl.WIconStartup: true|false
    dbxl.WScrollbars: vertical|horizontal|both|none

Formats is the menu that opens when you left-click a variable. "Save
layout" in the Options menu writes these resources for the current layout.


D. Notes on using dbxl
----------------------

    - dbxl shows the values of variables at the moment the program
      stopped; they are fetched again after every stop.
    - At the first line of a subprogram (before its prologue has run), its
      parameters and local variables may not have their values yet.
    - Programs compiled without -g can be debugged, but only their machine
      instructions and registers can be shown.


E. Common problems
------------------

    The program is not being run.
        The program has ended. Left-click Restart to start it again.
    The source file was not found in the source search path.
        Add the file's directory with -I or "Edit source search path".
    The text is too small.
        Use -scale 2, or -scale auto.
